Here's exactly where `saveDescription` is used — in the chart embed's edit dialog:

```133:143:apps/frontend/src/components/side-panel/story-chart-embed.tsx
			{canEdit && edit && chart.rawTag && (
				<ChartConfigEditDialog
					open={isEditOpen}
					onOpenChange={setIsEditOpen}
					config={config}
					availableColumns={availableColumns}
					isSaving={edit.isSaving}
					onSave={(next) => edit.saveChart(chart.rawTag!, next)}
					description={edit.saveDescription}
				/>
			)}
```

The `edit` object here comes from the `useStoryChartEdit()` context, so `edit.saveDescription` resolves to whichever value the surrounding provider supplied.

Let me show you where that description then lands inside the dialog.

Here's the full chain of usage for `saveDescription`:

**1. Read from context** in `story-chart-embed.tsx`:

```99:99:apps/frontend/src/components/side-panel/story-chart-embed.tsx
	const edit = useStoryChartEdit();
```

**2. Passed as `description`** to the dialog (same file):

```134:143:apps/frontend/src/components/side-panel/story-chart-embed.tsx
			{canEdit && edit && chart.rawTag && (
				<ChartConfigEditDialog
					open={isEditOpen}
					onOpenChange={setIsEditOpen}
					config={config}
					availableColumns={availableColumns}
					isSaving={edit.isSaving}
					onSave={(next) => edit.saveChart(chart.rawTag!, next)}
					description={edit.saveDescription}
				/>
			)}
```

**3. Rendered in the dialog UI** (`ChartConfigEditDialog` lives in `display-chart-edit-dialog.tsx`), inside the header description:

```120:122:apps/frontend/src/components/tool-calls/display-chart-edit-dialog.tsx
					<DialogDescription className='text-sm text-muted-foreground font-medium'>
						{description}
					</DialogDescription>
```

So the value set in the context provider (`VERSION_SAVE_DESCRIPTION` or `EDITOR_SAVE_DESCRIPTION`) flows through `story-chart-embed.tsx` and ends up as the subtitle text under "Edit chart" in the chart edit dialog. Note the dialog also has its own default (`'Tweak the chart parameters.'`) for callers that don't pass one.
